#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""cilia-gh-shim — intercept `gh pr review`, read-PROXY read-class verbs,
pass through everything else.

Installed via the existing track_files mechanism at reviewer track launch
(services/cilia/src/routes/dispatch.rs). PATH-prepended so this wins over
/usr/bin/gh inside the reviewer pane.

Pure stdlib so no `pip install` step is needed on workers.

Layers, applied in order:

  1. `gh pr review`  → translated into cilia review-store MCP calls
     (register_review_concern / approve_pr). Unchanged from the original.

  1.2. `gh issue create` → ROUTED TO THE CILIA-NATIVE FORGE (#3460 forge
     independence, slice 1), NOT github.com. Cilia mints its own issue
     number (the native store is authoritative) after running a mechanical
     dedup search and REQUIRING an explicit not-duplicate attestation — see
     `POST /api/issues` in `services/cilia/src/routes/issues.rs`. The shim
     accepts a shim-only `--not-duplicate "reason"` flag (gh itself has no
     such flag) that becomes the attestation; omit it and the create is
     rejected with the surfaced candidate issues printed to stderr so the
     caller can retry with one.

     UNLIKE Layer 1.5 below, this is FAIL-CLOSED like Layer 1 (`pr review`):
     if the native forge is unreachable, or rejects the create (missing/blank
     attestation, malformed repo, empty title), the shim surfaces the error
     LOUDLY and exits non-zero — it never falls through to a raw `gh issue
     create`, because that would create the issue straight on GitHub and
     bypass dedup entirely. Interactive forms (`--web`/`-w`, or no `--title`
     at all — gh would prompt) pass through to real gh since the shim can't
     safely drive a TTY prompt.

     `gh issue new` — gh's own documented alias for `create` — is intercepted
     identically; falling through on that spelling would let a caller bypass
     dedup+attestation. Every other `gh issue` subcommand (`edit`, `close`,
     `comment`, `list`, …) is untouched passthrough in this slice.

     Flags the native forge doesn't implement yet (`--assignee`,
     `--milestone`, `--project`, `--template`, `--recover`) are rejected
     fail-closed with a clear error rather than silently dropped — dropping
     them would report success for an issue materially different from what
     was asked for.

  1.5. `gh issue view N` → SERVED BY CILIA (#1537) under a STRICT ALLOWLIST.
     Routed to the cilia issue store REST endpoint (GET /api/issues/<n>) which
     mirrors-on-read: cilia shells `gh` ONCE on a store miss, then serves its own
     copy — so the fleet stops hitting github.com for single-issue reads. This is
     the first step of cilia becoming the GitHub intermediary for issues, the
     issue analogue of Layer 1's `gh pr review` translation.

     The allowlist (#2418) is the load-bearing safety property: cilia serves the
     view ONLY when it can answer FAITHFULLY — `--json` is present AND every
     requested field is one cilia actually mirrors AND there is no `-c/--comments`
     or `--jq`/`--template` render form. ANY other form FAILS OPEN to real gh.
     A wrong answer with exit 0 (a silently-dropped `--json comments`, a
     human-rendered view replaced by raw JSON) is worse than a passthrough.

     UNLIKE Layer 1 it also fails OPEN on cilia trouble: creds unset, transport
     error, or non-2xx all fall through to real gh, because a read mirror must
     never block an agent on a hub hiccup.

     `gh issue list` is DELIBERATELY NOT served in slice-1 (#2418): it passes
     straight through to real gh. A permanent list mirror would freeze the fleet's
     "list issues → pick work" loop — new issues never appear, closed ones never
     drop — and the shim can't faithfully translate gh's many list filters
     (`--label`/`--author`/`--search`/…). List write-through/invalidation is a
     #1537 follow-up; until then live passthrough is the safe choice.

  2. READ-CLASS verbs (`pr view`, `pr checks`, `run view`, `run list`, and
     read-only GET `gh api`) → host-local READ PROXY (#2415). The fleet
     shares ONE gh token; uncordinated read bursts (agent CI-wait poll loops)
     trip GitHub's SECONDARY/abuse limit even while primary quota is healthy.
     The proxy:
       (a) CACHES the result keyed on the full normalized arg vector with a
           short TTL (~15s) — a fresh hit replays stdout, stderr AND exit code
           without calling real gh. CI/PR state does not change faster than the
           TTL, and exit code matters (`gh pr checks` returns nonzero on
           failing checks; callers branch on it).
       (b) SERIALIZES + PACES misses behind a host-local flock with a minimum
           inter-call delay, so concurrent agents can't fan out N simultaneous
           gh calls.
       (c) BACKS OFF on a 403 / secondary-limit, honoring Retry-After when
           present (else exponential, capped), retrying a bounded number of
           times before returning the error — instead of returning the 403
           straight into the agent's loop, which re-fires and amplifies the
           storm.

  3. Everything else (writes — pr merge/create, api POST/PATCH/DELETE, issue
     create/edit — and any unrecognized subcommand) → raw passthrough to real
     gh, UNCACHED. Correctness over cache-hit-rate: when in doubt, passthrough.

Env (set by dispatch.rs at track launch):
  CILIA_URL          — full /mcp endpoint URL (e.g. http://127.0.0.1:8093/mcp)
  CILIA_AUTH_TOKEN   — Bearer token (cilia state.auth_token)

Optional:
  CILIA_GH_SHIM_REAL_GH — override the real gh binary path
                          (default: /usr/bin/gh, falls back to PATH search)
  CILIA_GH_SHIM_DEBUG_LOG — append every dispatch decision here. Doubles as the
                          read-proxy TALLY: one line per invocation
                          (timestamp, subcommand, hit/miss/passthrough) so
                          calls/min can be measured. Default off = no logging.
  CILIA_GH_SHIM_CACHE_DIR  — read-cache dir (default
                          ${XDG_RUNTIME_DIR:-/tmp}/cilia-gh-shim-cache)
  CILIA_GH_SHIM_CACHE_TTL  — cache TTL seconds (default 15)
  CILIA_GH_SHIM_PACE_MS    — min inter-call delay on misses (default 300)
  CILIA_GH_SHIM_MAX_RETRIES   — 403-backoff bounded retries (default 4)
  CILIA_GH_SHIM_BACKOFF_BASE_MS / CILIA_GH_SHIM_BACKOFF_CAP_MS — backoff curve
                          for the EXPONENTIAL fallback (defaults 200 / 3000).
                          An explicit Retry-After is honored in FULL, not
                          capped by BACKOFF_CAP_MS.
  CILIA_GH_SHIM_MAX_SLEEP_MS — max single honored Retry-After (default 120000);
                          a longer Retry-After surfaces the 403 immediately
                          instead of blocking the agent.

Exit codes:
  0   — review translated to cilia and posted, OR passthrough succeeded
        (os.execv replaces the process, so the real gh's exit code is
        returned directly to the caller in that branch).
  1   — cilia MCP returned an error (transport failure, HTTP non-2xx,
        JSON-RPC error envelope, or result.isError in a 2xx body).
        Concerns may have been PARTIALLY filed before the failing call;
        do NOT re-run with the real gh, that would post to GitHub a
        second time.
  2   — required env var missing (CILIA_URL or CILIA_AUTH_TOKEN). The
        shim refuses to fall through to real gh in this state because
        bypassing cilia review is exactly the failure mode this layer
        defends against (RC-002 / fail-closed).
  >2  — uncaught Python exception. Indicates a bug in the shim itself;
        capture stderr and report to the operator.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import base64
import contextlib
import hashlib
import json
import os
import re
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys
import time
from pathlib import Path
from urllib import error, request
from urllib.parse import quote

try:
    import fcntl  # POSIX only (Linux/macOS workers); absent on Windows.
except ImportError:  # pragma: no cover - workers are POSIX
    fcntl = None

# Allow `from parser import parse_review_body` regardless of cwd.
sys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent))


def _locate_real_gh() -> str:
    """Find the real gh binary, avoiding ourselves on PATH."""
    explicit = os.environ.get("CILIA_GH_SHIM_REAL_GH")
    if explicit:
        return explicit
    if os.path.exists("/usr/bin/gh"):
        return "/usr/bin/gh"
    # Last-ditch: walk PATH skipping the dir that contains this shim, so we
    # don't recursively exec ourselves.
    self_dir = os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__))
    for entry in os.environ.get("PATH", "").split(os.pathsep):
        if not entry:
            continue
        if os.path.realpath(entry) == self_dir:
            continue
        candidate = os.path.join(entry, "gh")
        if os.path.isfile(candidate) and os.access(candidate, os.X_OK):
            return candidate
    # Surrender — let the OS surface ENOENT for clarity.
    return shutil.which("gh") or "/usr/bin/gh"


def _passthrough(real_gh: str, argv_tail: list[str]) -> int:
    """Exec the real gh with the original args. Replaces this process."""
    os.execv(real_gh, [real_gh] + argv_tail)


def handle_passthrough(args: list[str], real_gh: str) -> int:
    return _passthrough(real_gh, args)

def _get_mcp_result(url: str, token: str, name: str, arguments: dict) -> dict:
    payload = {
        "jsonrpc": "2.0",
        "id": 1,
        "method": "tools/call",
        "params": {"name": name, "arguments": arguments},
    }
    req = request.Request(
        url,
        data=json.dumps(payload).encode(),
        method="POST",
        headers={
            "Content-Type": "application/json",
            "Accept": "application/json",
            "Authorization": f"Bearer {token}",
        },
    )
    try:
        with request.urlopen(req, timeout=15) as resp:
            status = resp.status
            body = resp.read()
    except error.HTTPError as e:
        status = e.code
        body = e.read() or b""
    except Exception as e:
        raise RuntimeError(f"transport failure calling {name}: {e}") from e

    if not (200 <= status < 300):
        raise RuntimeError(
            f"HTTP {status} from cilia for {name}: {body.decode(errors='replace')[:200]}"
        )

    try:
        parsed = json.loads(body.decode("utf-8") or "{}")
    except json.JSONDecodeError as e:
        raise RuntimeError(f"non-JSON response from cilia for {name}: {e}") from e

    if "error" in parsed:
        err = parsed["error"]
        msg = err.get("message", "unknown JSON-RPC error") if isinstance(err, dict) else str(err)
        raise RuntimeError(f"JSON-RPC error from cilia for {name}: {msg}")

    result = parsed.get("result")
    if isinstance(result, dict) and result.get("isError"):
        content = result.get("content") or []
        msg = "unknown MCP error"
        if content and isinstance(content[0], dict):
            msg = content[0].get("text", msg)
        raise RuntimeError(f"cilia MCP tool {name} returned isError: {msg}")

    return result

# NOTE: T7's original per-verb stub handlers (handle_pr_view/checks/list,
# handle_issue_view/list, handle_run_list/view, handle_pr_create/merge/close)
# were dropped on the #2418 rebase: they only `handle_passthrough`d, and S2's
# landed layered dispatch already serves those same verbs for real — pr/run
# reads via the #2415 cached read-proxy (_READ_VERBS) and issue reads via the
# #2418 cilia issue-serve (_is_issue_serve). Re-routing them to cilia
# primitives (create_dispatch/dispatch_merge/archive_dispatch) is tracked for a
# later, behavior-bearing slice rather than shipped as no-op scaffold here.
def handle_api(args: list[str], real_gh: str) -> int | None:
    """Serve `gh api .../pulls/N/reviews` GETs from cilia MCP; return None when
    the path isn't MCP-serviceable so the caller falls through to the read
    proxy / passthrough (never bypasses S2's api-read caching)."""
    if not args:
        return None
    
    path = args[0]
    parts = path.strip("/").split("/")
    
    is_get = True
    for a in args:
        if a in ("-X", "--method", "-F", "-f", "--field", "--raw-field"):
            if a in ("-X", "--method"):
                try:
                    idx = args.index(a)
                    if idx + 1 < len(args) and args[idx + 1].upper() != "GET":
                        is_get = False
                except ValueError:
                    pass
            else:
                is_get = False

    if is_get and len(parts) >= 5 and parts[0] == "repos" and parts[3] == "pulls" and parts[-1] == "reviews":
        owner, repo, number = parts[1], parts[2], parts[4]
        pr_ref = f"github://{owner}/{repo}/{number}"
        
        cilia_url = os.environ.get("CILIA_URL")
        cilia_token = os.environ.get("CILIA_AUTH_TOKEN")
        
        if cilia_url and cilia_token:
            try:
                res = _get_mcp_result(cilia_url, cilia_token, "get_review_summary", {"pr_ref": pr_ref})
                content = res.get("content", [])
                if content and isinstance(content[0], dict):
                    try:
                        cilia_data = json.loads(content[0].get("text", "{}"))
                    except json.JSONDecodeError:
                        cilia_data = {}
                    
                    verdict = cilia_data.get("verdict", "pending")
                    gh_state = "PENDING"
                    if verdict == "approve":
                        gh_state = "APPROVED"
                    elif verdict == "request_changes":
                        gh_state = "CHANGES_REQUESTED"
                    elif verdict == "comment":
                        gh_state = "COMMENTED"
                    
                    gh_reviews = []
                    if verdict != "pending":
                        gh_reviews.append({
                            "id": 1,
                            "user": {"login": "cilia-agent"},
                            "body": "Synthetic review from Cilia MCP",
                            "state": gh_state
                        })
                    
                    print(json.dumps(gh_reviews))
                    return 0
            except RuntimeError as e:
                _debug_log(f"API intercept failed: {e}")
                pass

    # Not MCP-serviceable (non-reviews path, write, or cilia unreachable):
    # signal the caller to fall through to the read proxy / passthrough.
    return None

def main(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    real_gh = _locate_real_gh()
    tail = argv[1:]

    # Layer 1: `gh pr review` interception (unchanged).
    if len(argv) >= 3 and argv[1] == "pr" and argv[2] == "review":
        return handle_pr_review(argv[3:], real_gh)

    # Layer 1.2 (#3460 slice 1): `gh issue create` ROUTED TO THE CILIA-NATIVE
    # FORGE instead of github.com. FAIL-CLOSED like Layer 1 (never falls
    # through to real gh on a native-forge error) — see module docstring.
    if _is_issue_create(tail):
        return handle_issue_create(tail[2:], real_gh)

    # Layer 1.5: `gh issue view N` SERVED BY CILIA, not github.com (#1537), under
    # the #2418 allowlist. `gh issue list` is NOT served (it passes through to
    # real gh below). `handle_issue` fails OPEN to real gh for any view form
    # cilia can't answer faithfully, and on any cilia trouble.
    if _is_issue_serve(tail):
        return handle_issue(tail, real_gh)

    # Layer 1.7 (T7 #2396): `gh api repos/{o}/{r}/pulls/{n}/reviews` GET SERVED
    # BY CILIA MCP (forge-native review summary). handle_api returns None when
    # the path isn't MCP-serviceable (non-reviews api, write, or cilia
    # unreachable), so we fall through to the read proxy below — preserving
    # S2's (#2415) caching for every other api read instead of bypassing it.
    if tail and tail[0] == "api":
        served = handle_api(tail[1:], real_gh)
        if served is not None:
            return served

    # Layer 2: read-class verbs go through the host-local read proxy (#2415).
    if _is_cacheable_read(tail):
        return handle_read(tail, real_gh)

    # Layer 3: writes + anything unrecognized → raw passthrough, never cached.
    _tally(_subcommand_label(tail), "passthrough")
    return _passthrough(real_gh, tail)


def _debug_log(msg: str) -> None:
    path = os.environ.get("CILIA_GH_SHIM_DEBUG_LOG")
    if not path:
        return
    try:
        with open(path, "a", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            fh.write(msg.rstrip() + "\n")
    except OSError:
        pass


def _subcommand_label(tail: list[str]) -> str:
    """A short 'verb subverb' label for the tally, ignoring flags/operands."""
    words = [a for a in tail[:3] if not a.startswith("-")]
    return " ".join(words[:2]) if words else "gh"


def _tally(subcommand: str, outcome: str) -> None:
    """Opt-in per-invocation tally line: '<unix-ts>\\t<subcommand>\\t<outcome>'.

    Reuses CILIA_GH_SHIM_DEBUG_LOG. Default off (no env) → zero behavior change.
    Lets us measure calls/min and cache hit-rate after the fact.
    """
    path = os.environ.get("CILIA_GH_SHIM_DEBUG_LOG")
    if not path:
        return
    try:
        with open(path, "a", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            fh.write(f"{time.time():.3f}\t{subcommand}\t{outcome}\n")
    except OSError:
        pass


# --- read-class classification ---------------------------------------------
# Subcommands whose output is a snapshot of remote state safe to cache briefly.
#
# Slice-1 scope is deliberately the CI-poll hot path that trips the secondary
# limit (`pr view` / `pr checks` / `run view` / `run list` + read-only
# `gh api`). `gh issue view` is NOT in this #2415 read-proxy set — it is handled
# one layer up by the cilia issue-serve path (#1537/#2418, `_is_issue_serve` /
# `handle_issue`), which mirrors it in cilia rather than caching+pacing a github
# passthrough. `gh issue list` is served by NEITHER layer in slice-1 (#2418): it
# is a raw Layer-3 passthrough so the fleet always sees live repo activity. Other
# reads — e.g. `gh pr diff` — remain INTENTIONALLY out of scope and pass through
# uncached/unpaced (RC-009).
_READ_VERBS = {
    ("pr", "view"),
    ("pr", "checks"),
    ("run", "view"),
    ("run", "list"),
}
# Flags that make a read interactive/streaming/side-effecting — never cache
# these; fall through to real gh so the live behavior is preserved.
_NONCACHEABLE_READ_FLAGS = {"--watch", "--web", "-w"}


def _is_read_only_api(api_args: list[str]) -> bool:
    """True for a GET `gh api` with no write fields.

    Mutating tells: an explicit non-GET `--method/-X`, any field flag
    (`-f/--field/-F/--raw-field`), or `--input` (request body). Anything we
    can't confidently classify as a pure GET → False (passthrough uncached).

    Field flags appear in BOTH detached (`-f key=value`) and ATTACHED
    (`-fkey=value` / `-Fkey=value`) short forms — gh accepts both. The
    attached form would otherwise slip past the bare-flag check below and a
    mutating POST (`gh api repos/... -ftitle=x`) would be misclassified as a
    cacheable GET (RC-002).
    """
    method = "GET"
    i = 0
    while i < len(api_args):
        a = api_args[i]
        if a in ("-X", "--method"):
            if i + 1 < len(api_args):
                method = api_args[i + 1].upper()
            i += 2
            continue
        if a.startswith("--method="):
            method = a.split("=", 1)[1].upper()
        elif a.startswith("-X") and len(a) > 2:  # attached: -XPOST
            method = a[2:].upper()
        elif a in ("-f", "--field", "-F", "--raw-field", "--input"):
            return False
        elif a.startswith(("--field=", "--raw-field=", "--input=")):
            return False
        # Attached short field flags: `-fkey=value` / `-Fkey=value`. Length
        # check excludes the bare `-f`/`-F` already handled above.
        elif len(a) > 2 and (a.startswith("-f") or a.startswith("-F")):
            return False
        i += 1
    return method == "GET"


def _is_cacheable_read(tail: list[str]) -> bool:
    """Is this invocation a read-class verb the proxy should cache/pace?"""
    if not tail:
        return False
    if any(f in _NONCACHEABLE_READ_FLAGS for f in tail):
        return False
    if (tail[0], tail[1] if len(tail) > 1 else "") in _READ_VERBS:
        return True
    if tail[0] == "api":
        return _is_read_only_api(tail[1:])
    return False


# --- cache + pacing + backoff ----------------------------------------------
def _cache_dir() -> str:
    base = os.environ.get("CILIA_GH_SHIM_CACHE_DIR")
    if not base:
        runtime = os.environ.get("XDG_RUNTIME_DIR") or "/tmp"  # noqa: S108
        base = os.path.join(runtime, "cilia-gh-shim-cache")
    return base


def _ensure_cache_dir() -> str:
    """Create the cache dir owner-only (0o700) and return its path.

    The dir holds cached PRIVATE gh responses. On the /tmp fallback a
    umask-created dir is world-readable (observed 0o775 dir / 0o664 files),
    leaking another user's PR data on a shared host (RC-008 / RC-013). We set
    the mode explicitly rather than trusting the process umask, and chmod an
    already-existing dir so a previously-loose dir is tightened too. makedirs'
    `mode` is itself masked by umask, so the follow-up chmod is load-bearing.
    """
    cdir = _cache_dir()
    os.makedirs(cdir, mode=0o700, exist_ok=True)
    with contextlib.suppress(OSError):
        os.chmod(cdir, 0o700)
    return cdir


def _cache_ttl() -> float:
    try:
        return float(os.environ.get("CILIA_GH_SHIM_CACHE_TTL", "15"))
    except ValueError:
        return 15.0


def _command_has_explicit_repo(tail: list[str]) -> bool:
    """True if the invocation names its repo explicitly via `-R`/`--repo`.

    When the repo is explicit the result is independent of which worktree the
    call runs from, so the cache key must NOT fold cwd (else identical explicit
    reads from different worktrees all miss the shared cache — RC-012).
    """
    for a in tail:
        if a in ("-R", "--repo"):
            return True
        if a.startswith("--repo=") or (a.startswith("-R") and len(a) > 2):
            return True
    return False


def _cache_key(tail: list[str]) -> str:
    """sha256 over the arg vector + the inputs that actually determine the
    repo a read resolves against.

    cwd is folded in ONLY when the read depends on local repo context — i.e.
    no `-R/--repo` flag AND no `GH_REPO`/`GITHUB_REPOSITORY` env (RC-012). A
    repo-relative read (`gh pr checks` with no --repo) resolves against the
    current worktree, so the same argv in two worktrees is two distinct
    results; but an explicit-repo read is worktree-independent and SHOULD share
    one cache entry across worktrees (cross-agent dedup is the point of the
    proxy). `GH_REPO`/`GITHUB_REPOSITORY` is folded in whenever set so that
    `GH_REPO=r1 gh pr view 1` and `GH_REPO=r2 gh pr view 1` in the same dir
    don't collide (RC-003).
    """
    gh_repo = os.environ.get("GH_REPO") or os.environ.get("GITHUB_REPOSITORY")
    repo_determined = _command_has_explicit_repo(tail) or bool(gh_repo)
    parts: list = [tail]
    parts.append({"GH_REPO": gh_repo} if gh_repo else None)
    parts.append(None if repo_determined else {"cwd": os.getcwd()})
    payload = json.dumps(parts, separators=(",", ":"))
    return hashlib.sha256(payload.encode("utf-8")).hexdigest()


def _cache_read(key: str) -> tuple[int, bytes, bytes] | None:
    """Return (rc, stdout, stderr) if a fresh entry exists, else None."""
    path = os.path.join(_cache_dir(), key)
    try:
        age = time.time() - os.path.getmtime(path)
        if age > _cache_ttl():
            return None
        with open(path, "rb") as fh:
            rec = json.loads(fh.read().decode("utf-8"))
        return (
            int(rec["rc"]),
            base64.b64decode(rec["out"]),
            base64.b64decode(rec["err"]),
        )
    except (OSError, ValueError, KeyError):
        return None


def _cache_write(key: str, rc: int, out: bytes, err: bytes) -> None:
    try:
        cdir = _ensure_cache_dir()
        rec = {
            "rc": rc,
            "out": base64.b64encode(out).decode("ascii"),
            "err": base64.b64encode(err).decode("ascii"),
        }
        tmp = os.path.join(cdir, f".{key}.{os.getpid()}.tmp")
        # Create owner-only (0o600); the cache record contains private gh
        # response bodies (RC-008 / RC-013). os.open with an explicit mode is
        # not subject to the default-664 umask the way open() is.
        fd = os.open(tmp, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_TRUNC, 0o600)
        try:
            with os.fdopen(fd, "wb") as fh:
                fh.write(json.dumps(rec).encode("utf-8"))
        finally:
            with contextlib.suppress(OSError):
                os.chmod(tmp, 0o600)  # tighten if umask/O_CREAT left it looser
        os.replace(tmp, os.path.join(cdir, key))
    except OSError:
        pass  # fail open — a cache write failure must never break the call


@contextlib.contextmanager
def _proxy_lock():
    """Host-local flock serializing read-class gh calls. Fails OPEN (yields a
    no-op) where flock is unavailable or the lock file can't be opened — same
    flock idiom as tools/cilia-build-shim/build-shim.sh.
    """
    if fcntl is None:
        yield
        return
    fh = None
    try:
        cdir = _ensure_cache_dir()
        fh = open(os.path.join(cdir, ".lock"), "a+")
        fcntl.flock(fh.fileno(), fcntl.LOCK_EX)
    except OSError:
        if fh is not None:
            fh.close()
        yield
        return
    try:
        yield
    finally:
        try:
            fcntl.flock(fh.fileno(), fcntl.LOCK_UN)
        finally:
            fh.close()


def _pace() -> None:
    """Enforce a minimum delay since the last real gh call (host-local).

    Called while holding the proxy lock, so all read-class misses across
    concurrent agents are serialized AND spaced by at least CILIA_GH_SHIM_PACE_MS.
    """
    try:
        pace_s = float(os.environ.get("CILIA_GH_SHIM_PACE_MS", "300")) / 1000.0
    except ValueError:
        pace_s = 0.3
    stamp = os.path.join(_cache_dir(), ".last-call")
    now = time.time()
    try:
        wait = pace_s - (now - os.path.getmtime(stamp))
        if wait > 0:
            time.sleep(wait)
    except OSError:
        pass  # no prior call recorded — no need to wait
    try:
        with open(stamp, "a"):
            os.utime(stamp, None)
    except OSError:
        pass


def _sweep_expired() -> None:
    """Opportunistic GC of cache entries older than the TTL (RC-007).

    Cheap: a single directory scan, called while the proxy lock is already
    held. Only removes cache-key files (hex sha256 names); skips the control
    files (`.lock`, `.last-call`) and in-flight `.<key>.<pid>.tmp` writes — all
    of which start with a dot. A stale entry surviving past TTL is only a
    disk-space concern (#1223), so any failure here is swallowed.
    """
    cdir = _cache_dir()
    ttl = _cache_ttl()
    now = time.time()
    try:
        names = os.listdir(cdir)
    except OSError:
        return
    for name in names:
        if name.startswith("."):
            continue
        path = os.path.join(cdir, name)
        try:
            if now - os.path.getmtime(path) > ttl:
                os.remove(path)
        except OSError:
            pass


# --- rate-limit / 403 classification ---------------------------------------
# Phrases that unambiguously denote GitHub's SECONDARY/abuse throttle — these
# are the only failures worth retrying under backoff.
_RATE_LIMIT_RE = re.compile(
    r"secondary rate limit"
    r"|api rate limit (?:already )?exceeded"
    r"|you have triggered an abuse",
    re.IGNORECASE,
)
# A bare 'HTTP 403' is NOT on its own a rate-limit: a missing-PR `gh pr view`,
# a SAML/SSO-enforcement 403, or a token-scope 403 are all permanent failures
# that must NOT be retried 4x under the lock (RC-005 / RC-006). Treat an
# 'HTTP 403' as rate-limited ONLY when a throttle co-token also appears.
# Mirrors the Rust side's tighter 'http 403' intent.
_HTTP_403_RE = re.compile(r"\bhttp[ /]?403\b|\b403 forbidden\b", re.IGNORECASE)
_RATE_COTOKEN_RE = re.compile(
    r"rate limit|abuse|retry[- ]after|too many requests", re.IGNORECASE
)
_RETRY_AFTER_RE = re.compile(r"retry[- ]after[:= ]\s*(\d+)", re.IGNORECASE)


def _is_rate_limited(rc: int, stderr: bytes) -> bool:
    if rc == 0:
        return False
    s = stderr.decode("utf-8", "replace")
    if _RATE_LIMIT_RE.search(s):
        return True
    return bool(_HTTP_403_RE.search(s) and _RATE_COTOKEN_RE.search(s))


def _backoff_params() -> tuple[int, float, float, float]:
    """(retries, base_s, cap_s, max_sleep_s) from env, with safe fallbacks."""
    try:
        retries = int(os.environ.get("CILIA_GH_SHIM_MAX_RETRIES", "4"))
        base_s = float(os.environ.get("CILIA_GH_SHIM_BACKOFF_BASE_MS", "200")) / 1000.0
        cap_s = float(os.environ.get("CILIA_GH_SHIM_BACKOFF_CAP_MS", "3000")) / 1000.0
        # Upper bound on a single honored Retry-After. Beyond this we'd rather
        # surface the 403 immediately than block the agent for minutes.
        max_sleep_s = float(os.environ.get("CILIA_GH_SHIM_MAX_SLEEP_MS", "120000")) / 1000.0
    except ValueError:
        retries, base_s, cap_s, max_sleep_s = 4, 0.2, 3.0, 120.0
    return retries, base_s, cap_s, max_sleep_s


def _run_real_gh(real_gh: str, tail: list[str]) -> tuple[int, bytes, bytes]:
    # NOTE: read-class verbs run via subprocess with captured (piped, non-TTY)
    # stdout/stderr — UNLIKE the os.execv passthrough path which preserves the
    # caller's TTY. Read-class verbs are therefore always non-interactive
    # (RC-010); interactive/streaming forms (--watch/--web) are excluded from
    # the read set in `_is_cacheable_read` and fall through to execv.
    proc = subprocess.run([real_gh, *tail], capture_output=True)  # noqa: S603
    return proc.returncode, proc.stdout, proc.stderr


def _next_backoff_sleep(
    err: bytes, delay: float, cap_s: float, max_sleep_s: float
) -> float | None:
    """Seconds to sleep before the next retry, or None to give up now.

    Honors the FULL `Retry-After` when present (RC-001) — the cap applies only
    to the exponential fallback. If an explicit Retry-After exceeds our budget
    (`max_sleep_s`), return None so the caller surfaces the 403 immediately
    instead of hammering / burning all retries on a too-short sleep.
    """
    m = _RETRY_AFTER_RE.search(err.decode("utf-8", "replace"))
    if m:
        retry_after = float(m.group(1))
        if retry_after > max_sleep_s:
            _debug_log(
                f"Retry-After {retry_after:.0f}s exceeds budget "
                f"{max_sleep_s:.0f}s; returning 403 without retry"
            )
            return None
        return retry_after  # honor it in full
    return min(delay, cap_s)  # cap only the exponential fallback


def _replay(rc: int, out: bytes, err: bytes) -> int:
    sys.stdout.buffer.write(out)
    sys.stdout.buffer.flush()
    sys.stderr.buffer.write(err)
    sys.stderr.buffer.flush()
    return rc


def handle_read(tail: list[str], real_gh: str) -> int:
    """Read proxy: cache hit → replay; miss → serialize+pace+backoff, cache, replay.

    Read-class verbs always run NON-INTERACTIVELY (piped subprocess, see
    `_run_real_gh` / RC-010).

    The backoff SLEEP happens with the proxy lock RELEASED (RC-004): each gh
    attempt acquires the lock, re-checks the cache, paces, and runs exactly
    once, then drops the lock before sleeping. Without this, one retrying call
    would hold the exclusive lock for the whole backoff window and head-of-line
    block every other read-class gh call on the host — exactly under the
    secondary-limit storm this proxy is meant to absorb.
    """
    label = _subcommand_label(tail)
    key = _cache_key(tail)

    hit = _cache_read(key)
    if hit is not None:
        _tally(label, "hit")
        return _replay(*hit)

    retries, base_s, cap_s, max_sleep_s = _backoff_params()
    delay = base_s
    attempt = 0
    while True:
        with _proxy_lock():
            _sweep_expired()  # opportunistic GC while holding the lock (RC-007)
            # Re-check under the lock: a sibling racing us (or filling it during
            # our backoff sleep) may have just written the entry.
            hit = _cache_read(key)
            if hit is not None:
                _tally(label, "hit")
                return _replay(*hit)
            _pace()
            rc, out, err = _run_real_gh(real_gh, tail)
            if not _is_rate_limited(rc, err):
                # Cache successes and non-transient failures (failing checks →
                # rc!=0 is a real, cacheable answer). Never cache a still-rate-
                # limited result: it's transient and caching would suppress
                # recovery for the whole TTL.
                _cache_write(key, rc, out, err)
                _tally(label, "miss")
                return _replay(rc, out, err)
        # --- lock RELEASED before the backoff sleep (RC-004) ---
        attempt += 1
        if attempt > max(0, retries):
            break
        sleep_s = _next_backoff_sleep(err, delay, cap_s, max_sleep_s)
        if sleep_s is None:
            break  # Retry-After over budget → surface the 403 now (RC-001)
        _debug_log(f"403/secondary-limit backoff: sleeping {sleep_s:.2f}s before retry")
        time.sleep(sleep_s)
        delay *= 2

    _tally(label, "miss")
    return _replay(rc, out, err)


# --- issue create (#3460 slice 1) -------------------------------------------
# `gh issue create` is routed to the cilia-native forge instead of github.com.
# Unlike issue-serve below, this is FAIL-CLOSED: any native-forge trouble
# (unreachable, or a real rejection) surfaces loudly and exits non-zero —
# NEVER falls through to a raw `gh issue create`, which would create the issue
# straight on GitHub and bypass the dedup+attestation gate entirely.

# `--web`/`-w` opens a browser to draft interactively; the shim can't drive that.
_ISSUE_CREATE_NONCACHEABLE_FLAGS = {"--web", "-w"}

# `new` is gh's own documented alias for `create` (`gh issue create --help`
# lists it under Aliases). Not covering it here would let `gh issue new`
# create straight on GitHub, bypassing the dedup+attestation gate entirely.
_ISSUE_CREATE_SUBCOMMANDS = {"create", "new"}


def _is_issue_create(tail: list[str]) -> bool:
    """Is this a `gh issue create` (or its `new` alias) the shim should route
    to the native forge?"""
    if len(tail) < 2 or tail[0] != "issue" or tail[1] not in _ISSUE_CREATE_SUBCOMMANDS:
        return False
    return not any(f in _ISSUE_CREATE_NONCACHEABLE_FLAGS for f in tail)


def _http_post_json(url: str, token: str, payload: dict) -> tuple[int, bytes]:
    """POST `payload` as JSON with a Bearer token. Returns (status, body).
    Raises on a transport failure (caller treats that as a loud, non-zero
    exit — never a silent fallback to real gh for a write)."""
    req = request.Request(
        url,
        data=json.dumps(payload).encode("utf-8"),
        method="POST",
        headers={
            "Content-Type": "application/json",
            "Accept": "application/json",
            "Authorization": f"Bearer {token}",
        },
    )
    with request.urlopen(req, timeout=15) as resp:
        return resp.status, resp.read()


def handle_issue_create(args: list[str], real_gh: str) -> int:
    """Route `gh issue create` to `POST /api/issues` on the cilia-native forge.

    Accepts the real gh flags (`-t/--title`, `-b/--body`, `-F/--body-file`,
    `-l/--label` repeatable, `-R/--repo`) plus a shim-only `--not-duplicate
    "reason"` that becomes the create's `not_duplicate_attestation`. `gh`
    itself has no such flag — an agent that omits it gets the create rejected
    with the surfaced near-duplicate candidates printed to stderr, so it can
    retry with one.

    FAILS CLOSED (see module docstring, Layer 1.2): every failure path here
    (interactive, missing creds, transport error, or a native-forge rejection)
    either passes through for a form the shim genuinely can't drive (`--web`,
    no `--title` at all) or exits non-zero WITHOUT touching real gh. A create
    that silently fell back to raw `gh issue create` on any of these would
    bypass dedup — exactly the failure mode this layer exists to prevent.
    """
    label = "issue create"
    p = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog="cilia-gh-shim issue create", add_help=False)
    p.add_argument("-t", "--title")
    p.add_argument("-b", "--body")
    p.add_argument("-F", "--body-file")
    p.add_argument("-l", "--label", action="append", default=[])
    p.add_argument("-R", "--repo")
    p.add_argument("-w", "--web", action="store_true")
    p.add_argument("--not-duplicate", dest="not_duplicate")
    try:
        parsed, unknown = p.parse_known_args(args)
    except SystemExit:
        # argparse rejected the args (e.g., -h) — let real gh handle it.
        return _passthrough(real_gh, ["issue", "create"] + args)

    if parsed.web:
        # Already filtered by `_is_issue_create`, but defensive in case a
        # future caller invokes this directly.
        return _passthrough(real_gh, ["issue", "create"] + args)

    if not parsed.title:
        # No `--title` at all means gh would prompt interactively — the shim
        # can't safely drive that, so let real gh handle it.
        _debug_log(f"{label}: falling through (no --title, interactive mode): {args}")
        return _passthrough(real_gh, ["issue", "create"] + args)

    if unknown:
        # Real `gh issue create` also supports -a/--assignee, -m/--milestone,
        # -p/--project, -T/--template, --recover, --editor — none of which the
        # native-forge create path implements yet. Silently accepting and
        # dropping them (the old `parse_known_args` behavior) would create a
        # materially different issue than the caller asked for. Fail closed
        # instead of passing through (passthrough would create straight on
        # GitHub and bypass dedup) and instead of silently ignoring them.
        sys.stderr.write(
            f"cilia-gh-shim: issue create does not yet support: {' '.join(unknown)}\n"
            "The native-forge create path only implements -t/--title, -b/--body, "
            "-F/--body-file, -l/--label (repeatable), -R/--repo, -w/--web, and the "
            "shim-only --not-duplicate. Refusing to silently drop the semantics of "
            "unsupported flags (e.g. --assignee, --milestone, --project, --template, "
            "--recover) — that would create a different issue than requested.\n"
        )
        _tally(label, "unsupported_flags")
        return 1

    body_text = parsed.body
    if parsed.body_file:
        if parsed.body_file == "-":
            # Real gh treats `-F -`/`--body-file -` as "read the body from
            # stdin" (a common `echo ... | gh issue create -F -` idiom).
            body_text = sys.stdin.read()
        else:
            try:
                body_text = Path(parsed.body_file).read_text()
            except OSError as e:
                sys.stderr.write(f"cilia-gh-shim: cannot read --body-file {parsed.body_file}: {e}\n")
                return 1

    cilia_url = os.environ.get("CILIA_URL")
    cilia_token = os.environ.get("CILIA_AUTH_TOKEN")
    if not cilia_url or not cilia_token:
        # Fail-closed (mirrors Layer 1's RC-002): this layer exists to
        # guarantee issue creation goes through the native dedup+attestation
        # gate. Falling through to real gh here would create straight on
        # GitHub and defeat the whole point.
        sys.stderr.write(
            "cilia-gh-shim: refusing to create the issue on GitHub directly because CILIA_URL "
            "or CILIA_AUTH_TOKEN is unset. This shim exists to route issue creation through the "
            "native forge's dedup+attestation gate. Fix the worker's MCP credentials (see "
            "services/remote-agent/wrappers/) and retry.\n"
        )
        return 2

    base = _cilia_api_base()
    if not base:
        sys.stderr.write("cilia-gh-shim: could not derive the cilia REST base from CILIA_URL.\n")
        return 2

    payload = {
        "title": parsed.title,
        "body": body_text,
        "labels": parsed.label,
        "not_duplicate_attestation": parsed.not_duplicate,
    }
    if parsed.repo:
        payload["repo"] = parsed.repo

    try:
        status, body = _http_post_json(f"{base}/api/issues", cilia_token, payload)
    except error.HTTPError as e:
        status = e.code
        body = e.read() or b""
    except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001 — any transport failure is LOUD, never a silent fallback
        sys.stderr.write(
            f"cilia-gh-shim: native forge unreachable while creating issue: {e}\n"
            "Refusing to fall back to a raw `gh issue create` — that would bypass dedup.\n"
        )
        _tally(label, "error")
        return 1

    if 200 <= status < 300:
        try:
            created = json.loads(body.decode("utf-8"))
        except (ValueError, UnicodeDecodeError):
            created = {}
        number = created.get("number")
        gh_mirror = created.get("gh_mirror") or {}
        url = gh_mirror.get("url") or f"{base.replace('http://', 'cilia://').replace('https://', 'cilia://')}/issues/{number}"
        print(url)
        _debug_log(f"{label}: created native issue #{number} ({url})")
        _tally(label, "created")
        return 0

    # Any non-2xx — including 409 (missing/blank attestation) and 400
    # (malformed repo/empty title) — is a REAL rejection, surfaced loudly.
    # Never passthrough here: that would create the issue straight on GitHub,
    # bypassing the dedup+attestation gate this layer exists to enforce.
    try:
        err = json.loads(body.decode("utf-8"))
    except (ValueError, UnicodeDecodeError):
        err = {"error": body.decode("utf-8", "replace")}
    sys.stderr.write(f"cilia-gh-shim: issue create rejected (HTTP {status}): {err.get('error', err)}\n")
    candidates = err.get("dedup_candidates") or []
    if candidates:
        sys.stderr.write("Near-duplicate candidates surfaced by the dedup search:\n")
        for c in candidates:
            sys.stderr.write(f"  #{c.get('number')} (score={c.get('score'):.2f}): {c.get('title')}\n")
        sys.stderr.write(
            'Retry with --not-duplicate "reason this is not a duplicate of the above" '
            "to proceed.\n"
        )
    _tally(label, "rejected")
    return 1


# --- issue serve (#1537 / #2418) --------------------------------------------
# `gh issue view N` is served by the cilia issue store instead of github.com,
# under a strict allowlist. The shim GETs the cilia REST endpoint; cilia
# mirrors-on-read (shells real gh once on a store miss, serves its copy after).
# `gh issue list` is NOT served in slice-1 — it passes straight through.

# `--web`/`-w` open a browser (interactive); cilia can't serve those.
_ISSUE_NONCACHEABLE_FLAGS = {"--web", "-w"}
# `gh issue view` flags that consume the following token as their VALUE, so a
# value is never mistaken for the positional issue number.
_ISSUE_VALUE_FLAGS = {"-R", "--repo", "--json", "--jq", "-q", "--template", "-t"}
# `--jq`/`--template` ask gh to render the result locally; we can't replicate
# that over the JSON the cilia endpoint returns, so those forms pass through.
_ISSUE_RENDER_FLAGS = {"--jq", "-q", "--template", "-t"}
# `gh issue view --comments`/`-c` asks for the comment thread, which cilia
# deliberately omits from its mirror (ISSUE_VIEW_FIELDS) — serving the cilia copy
# would silently drop comments, so this form passes through (#2418 RC-003).
_ISSUE_COMMENTS_FLAGS = {"-c", "--comments"}
# The issue `--json` fields cilia's mirror actually stores. MUST stay in sync
# with ISSUE_VIEW_FIELDS in services/cilia/src/gh.rs. A `--json` request is
# served from cilia ONLY when every requested field is in this set; a field
# cilia doesn't mirror (comments, id, closed, projectCards, projectItems,
# reactionGroups, …) means cilia can't answer faithfully → fail OPEN to real gh
# rather than serve a silently-incomplete projection (#2418 RC-007/012).
_CILIA_MIRRORED_ISSUE_FIELDS = frozenset(
    {
        "number",
        "title",
        "state",
        "body",
        "author",
        "labels",
        "assignees",
        "milestone",
        "createdAt",
        "updatedAt",
        "closedAt",
        "url",
    }
)


def _is_issue_serve(tail: list[str]) -> bool:
    """Is this a `gh issue view` the shim should TRY to serve from cilia?

    Only `issue view` is a candidate (#2418): `gh issue list` is never served in
    slice-1 (it passes through so the fleet sees live repo activity). `--web`/`-w`
    open a browser, so they pass through too. `handle_issue` then applies the rest
    of the allowlist and fails OPEN to real gh for any form cilia can't faithfully
    serve.
    """
    if len(tail) < 2 or tail[0] != "issue" or tail[1] != "view":
        return False
    return not any(f in _ISSUE_NONCACHEABLE_FLAGS for f in tail)


def _cilia_api_base() -> str | None:
    """The cilia REST base derived from CILIA_URL (its `/mcp` endpoint).

    CILIA_URL points at the JSON-RPC MCP endpoint — either the plain `/mcp`
    (e.g. `http://127.0.0.1:8093/mcp`) or, for sessions opted into aggregate
    routing (`route_mcp_aggregate`, #2440), `/mcp/aggregate`. The issue store
    lives under `/api/...` on the same origin in BOTH cases — strip whichever
    suffix is present to get there.

    Bug fixed here (mobile-companion escalation, verified via direct repro):
    only stripping a literal trailing `/mcp` left `/mcp/aggregate` callers
    with a base of `.../mcp/aggregate`, so `POST {base}/api/issues` actually
    hit `.../mcp/aggregate/api/issues` — a real, different route that happens
    to exist and returns 405, not the 404 you'd expect from a pure typo. The
    405 masked the real bug as something router-shaped. Check the longer
    suffix first so `/mcp/aggregate` doesn't partially match as `/mcp`.
    """
    url = os.environ.get("CILIA_URL")
    if not url:
        return None
    base = url.rstrip("/")
    if base.endswith("/mcp/aggregate"):
        base = base[: -len("/mcp/aggregate")]
    elif base.endswith("/mcp"):
        base = base[: -len("/mcp")]
    return base


def _extract_repo(args: list[str]) -> str | None:
    """The repo from `-R`/`--repo` (detached, `--repo=`, or attached `-R`), or
    the `GH_REPO`/`GITHUB_REPOSITORY` env. `None` → cilia uses its default."""
    i = 0
    while i < len(args):
        a = args[i]
        if a in ("-R", "--repo"):
            return args[i + 1] if i + 1 < len(args) else None
        if a.startswith("--repo="):
            return a.split("=", 1)[1]
        if a.startswith("-R") and len(a) > 2:
            return a[2:]
        i += 1
    return os.environ.get("GH_REPO") or os.environ.get("GITHUB_REPOSITORY")


def _extract_json_fields(args: list[str]) -> list[str] | None:
    """The fields from `--json a,b` / `--json=a,b`, [] for a bare `--json`, or
    None when no `--json` flag is present (a human-format request)."""
    i = 0
    while i < len(args):
        a = args[i]
        if a == "--json":
            return [f for f in args[i + 1].split(",") if f] if i + 1 < len(args) else []
        if a.startswith("--json="):
            return [f for f in a.split("=", 1)[1].split(",") if f]
        i += 1
    return None


def _first_positional(args: list[str]) -> str | None:
    """First non-flag token, skipping value-flag operands."""
    i = 0
    while i < len(args):
        a = args[i]
        if a in _ISSUE_VALUE_FLAGS:
            i += 2
            continue
        if a.startswith("-"):
            i += 1
            continue
        return a
    return None


def _project_fields(body: bytes, fields: list[str]) -> bytes:
    """Subset a cilia issue / issue-list JSON to the caller's `--json` fields.

    The cilia endpoint returns the full canonical field set; a `--json a,b`
    caller asked for a subset, so we project to match what real gh would emit.
    On any parse surprise, return the body unchanged.
    """
    try:
        data = json.loads(body.decode("utf-8"))
    except (ValueError, UnicodeDecodeError):
        return body
    fset = set(fields)
    if isinstance(data, list):
        proj: object = [
            {k: v for k, v in row.items() if k in fset}
            for row in data
            if isinstance(row, dict)
        ]
    elif isinstance(data, dict):
        proj = {k: v for k, v in data.items() if k in fset}
    else:
        return body
    return json.dumps(proj).encode("utf-8")


def _http_get(url: str, token: str) -> tuple[int, bytes]:
    """GET `url` with a Bearer token. Returns (status, body). Raises on a
    transport failure (caller falls through to real gh)."""
    req = request.Request(
        url,
        method="GET",
        headers={"Accept": "application/json", "Authorization": f"Bearer {token}"},
    )
    try:
        with request.urlopen(req, timeout=15) as resp:
            return resp.status, resp.read()
    except error.HTTPError as e:
        return e.code, e.read() or b""


def _emit_served(body: bytes, fields: list[str] | None) -> int:
    out = _project_fields(body, fields) if fields is not None else body
    sys.stdout.buffer.write(out)
    if not out.endswith(b"\n"):
        sys.stdout.buffer.write(b"\n")
    sys.stdout.buffer.flush()
    return 0


def handle_issue(tail: list[str], real_gh: str) -> int:
    """Serve `gh issue view N` from cilia under the #2418 allowlist, falling
    through to real gh for ANY form cilia can't answer faithfully (fail OPEN).

    Served ONLY when ALL hold:
      * `--json` is present with at least one field (no/empty `--json` → gh
        renders human text we can't replicate, or errors → passthrough),
      * every requested `--json` field is in `_CILIA_MIRRORED_ISSUE_FIELDS`
        (an un-mirrored field like `comments` → passthrough, never a silently
        incomplete projection),
      * no `-c`/`--comments` (cilia doesn't mirror the comment thread),
      * no `--jq`/`--template` render form (needs gh's local engine),
      * cilia creds are present and the positional is a numeric issue id.
    A wrong answer with exit 0 is worse than a passthrough.
    """
    args = tail[2:]  # tail[:2] == ["issue", "view"]
    label = "issue view"

    def passthrough(reason: str) -> int:
        _debug_log(f"{label}: passthrough ({reason}): {args}")
        _tally(label, "passthrough")
        return _passthrough(real_gh, tail)

    # Render forms need real gh's local jq/template engine.
    if any(
        a in _ISSUE_RENDER_FLAGS or a.startswith(("--jq=", "--template="))
        for a in args
    ):
        return passthrough("jq/template render")

    # Comments aren't mirrored — serving cilia's copy would silently drop them.
    if any(a in _ISSUE_COMMENTS_FLAGS for a in args):
        return passthrough("--comments not mirrored")

    # `--json` is the machine-readable contract; without it (or with a bare
    # `--json`, which gh itself rejects) gh prints a human view we can't render.
    fields = _extract_json_fields(args)
    if not fields:
        return passthrough("no/empty --json (human render or gh error)")

    # Serve only when EVERY requested field is one cilia actually mirrors.
    unmirrored = [f for f in fields if f not in _CILIA_MIRRORED_ISSUE_FIELDS]
    if unmirrored:
        return passthrough(f"un-mirrored --json field(s) {unmirrored}")

    base = _cilia_api_base()
    token = os.environ.get("CILIA_AUTH_TOKEN")
    if not base or not token:
        # Fail OPEN: missing cilia creds here just means we serve from github
        # directly (the read mirror is best-effort, NOT fail-closed like review).
        return passthrough("CILIA_URL/CILIA_AUTH_TOKEN unset")

    number = _first_positional(args)
    if number is None or not number.isdigit():
        # URL / branch / no-number forms — let real gh resolve them.
        return passthrough("non-numeric issue ref")

    repo = _extract_repo(args)
    url = f"{base}/api/issues/{number}"
    if repo:
        url += f"?repo={quote(repo, safe='')}"

    try:
        status, body = _http_get(url, token)
    except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001 — any transport failure → passthrough
        return passthrough(f"cilia transport failure: {e}")

    if 200 <= status < 300:
        _debug_log(f"{label}: served by cilia ({url})")
        _tally(label, "served")
        return _emit_served(body, fields)

    if status == 404:
        # cilia's mirror says this issue genuinely doesn't exist — surface it
        # like real gh (nonzero exit), don't double-check github.
        sys.stderr.write(body.decode("utf-8", "replace").rstrip() + "\n")
        _debug_log(f"{label}: cilia 404 (issue not found)")
        _tally(label, "served")
        return 1

    # Any other non-2xx (5xx, 502 upstream gh failure, auth) → fall through so
    # a hub problem never strands the agent.
    return passthrough(f"cilia HTTP {status}")


def _call_mcp_tool(url: str, token: str, name: str, arguments: dict) -> None:
    """POST one JSON-RPC tools/call. Raises RuntimeError on any failure.

    Cilia's MCP `tools/call` returns failures inside HTTP 200 JSON-RPC bodies
    with `result.isError=true` or with a top-level `error` envelope (see
    services/cilia/src/routes/misc.rs + services/cilia/src/mcp.rs). HTTP 2xx
    alone is NOT enough to claim success — we must inspect both shapes.
    """
    payload = {
        "jsonrpc": "2.0",
        "id": 1,
        "method": "tools/call",
        "params": {"name": name, "arguments": arguments},
    }
    req = request.Request(
        url,
        data=json.dumps(payload).encode(),
        method="POST",
        headers={
            "Content-Type": "application/json",
            "Accept": "application/json",
            "Authorization": f"Bearer {token}",
        },
    )
    try:
        with request.urlopen(req, timeout=15) as resp:
            status = resp.status
            body = resp.read()
    except error.HTTPError as e:
        status = e.code
        body = e.read() or b""
    except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001
        raise RuntimeError(f"transport failure calling {name}: {e}") from e

    if not (200 <= status < 300):
        raise RuntimeError(
            f"HTTP {status} from cilia for {name}: {body.decode(errors='replace')[:200]}"
        )

    try:
        parsed = json.loads(body.decode("utf-8") or "{}")
    except json.JSONDecodeError as e:
        raise RuntimeError(f"non-JSON response from cilia for {name}: {e}") from e

    if "error" in parsed:
        err = parsed["error"]
        msg = err.get("message", "unknown JSON-RPC error") if isinstance(err, dict) else str(err)
        raise RuntimeError(f"JSON-RPC error from cilia for {name}: {msg}")

    result = parsed.get("result")
    if isinstance(result, dict) and result.get("isError"):
        content = result.get("content") or []
        msg = "unknown MCP error"
        if content and isinstance(content[0], dict):
            msg = content[0].get("text", msg)
        raise RuntimeError(f"cilia MCP tool {name} returned isError: {msg}")


def handle_pr_review(args: list[str], real_gh: str) -> int:
    """Translate `gh pr review` to cilia MCP calls.

    Falls through to the real gh whenever the invocation is interactive,
    repo-ambiguous, or otherwise outside the slice the shim handles. The
    fall-through is via os.execv so the operator sees identical behavior
    to running the real gh directly.
    """
    # Mirror the short forms `gh pr review` accepts (per `gh help pr review`):
    #   -a/--approve, -b/--body, -c/--comment, -F/--body-file,
    #   -r/--request-changes, -R/--repo. Skipping any of these lets the
    #   short-form invocation slip past `parse_known_args` and the shim
    #   falls through to real gh — the exact RC-001 bypass.
    p = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog="cilia-gh-shim pr review", add_help=False)
    p.add_argument("pr_number", type=int, nargs="?")
    p.add_argument("-R", "--repo")
    p.add_argument("-a", "--approve", action="store_true")
    p.add_argument("-r", "--request-changes", action="store_true")
    p.add_argument("-c", "--comment", action="store_true")
    p.add_argument("-b", "--body")
    p.add_argument("-F", "--body-file")
    try:
        parsed, _unknown = p.parse_known_args(args)
    except SystemExit:
        # argparse rejected the args (e.g., -h) — let real gh handle it.
        return _passthrough(real_gh, ["pr", "review"] + args)

    if parsed.approve:
        verdict = "approve"
    elif parsed.request_changes:
        verdict = "request_changes"
    elif parsed.comment:
        verdict = "comment"
    else:
        # Interactive mode (no verdict flag) — only real gh can drive this.
        _debug_log(f"falling through (no verdict flag): {args}")
        return _passthrough(real_gh, ["pr", "review"] + args)

    if not parsed.pr_number or not parsed.repo:
        # Repo-ambiguous: real gh resolves the current dir to a remote, we don't.
        _debug_log(f"falling through (no pr_number or --repo): {args}")
        return _passthrough(real_gh, ["pr", "review"] + args)

    body_text = parsed.body
    if parsed.body_file:
        try:
            body_text = Path(parsed.body_file).read_text()
        except OSError as e:
            sys.stderr.write(f"cilia-gh-shim: cannot read --body-file {parsed.body_file}: {e}\n")
            return 1

    cilia_url = os.environ.get("CILIA_URL")
    cilia_token = os.environ.get("CILIA_AUTH_TOKEN")
    if not cilia_url or not cilia_token:
        # RC-002: fail-closed. This shim's whole reason to exist is to
        # guarantee `gh pr review` lands in cilia even when the worker's
        # MCP wiring is broken. Falling through to real gh in that case
        # defeats the layer — review posts to GitHub, cilia never sees it.
        sys.stderr.write(
            "cilia-gh-shim: refusing to forward review to GitHub because CILIA_URL or "
            "CILIA_AUTH_TOKEN is unset. This shim exists to ensure reviews land in cilia. "
            "Fix the worker's MCP credentials (see services/remote-agent/wrappers/) and retry.\n"
        )
        return 2

    from parser import parse_review_body  # noqa: WPS433 (local import — sys.path set above)

    concerns = parse_review_body(body_text or "")
    pr_ref = f"github://{parsed.repo}/{parsed.pr_number}"

    # RC-003: any failure from _call_mcp_tool — transport, HTTP non-2xx,
    # JSON-RPC error, or isError-in-2xx — must exit non-zero WITHOUT
    # falling through to real gh. Concerns may have partially registered;
    # re-running real gh would post to GitHub a second time.
    try:
        for concern in concerns:
            _call_mcp_tool(
                cilia_url,
                cilia_token,
                "register_review_concern",
                {
                    "pr_ref": pr_ref,
                    "severity": concern["severity"],
                    "concern_text": concern["concern_text"],
                    "file_path": concern["file_path"],
                    "line_range": concern["line_range"],
                },
            )
            _debug_log("register_review_concern ok")

        _call_mcp_tool(
            cilia_url,
            cilia_token,
            "approve_pr",
            {"pr_ref": pr_ref, "verdict_override": verdict},
        )
        _debug_log("approve_pr ok")
    except RuntimeError as e:
        sys.stderr.write(f"cilia-gh-shim: {e}\n")
        return 1

    # Synthetic stdout shaped like real gh so any caller scraping output
    # gets a recognizable line. Only printed on full success.
    print(
        f"Reviewed pull request #{parsed.pr_number} ({verdict}) "
        f"— translated to {len(concerns)} cilia concern(s) via gh-shim"
    )
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv))
